WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.607
A report sums every payment over ninety euros, out of ten million rows.

2
00:00:07.607 --> 00:00:10.372
One comparison takes under a nanosecond.

3
00:00:10.567 --> 00:00:14.889
The database runs it as a plan, a tree of small operators.

4
00:00:14.889 --> 00:00:20.479
A scan reads the table, a filter keeps amounts over 9,000, a sum adds them.

5
00:00:20.633 --> 00:00:28.192
Each operator asks its child for the next row: the sum calls next on the filter, the filter calls next on the scan.

6
00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:32.141
The scan reads one payment and hands it up.

7
00:00:32.141 --> 00:00:35.446
4,200 fails, so the filter asks again.

8
00:00:35.446 --> 00:00:38.404
9,700 passes, and the sum adds it.

9
00:00:38.633 --> 00:00:41.351
Then the chain runs again for the next row.

10
00:00:41.351 --> 00:00:44.321
Every row costs a function call at every level.

11
00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:49.802
For ten million rows, the scan alone is called ten million times.

12
00:00:49.802 --> 00:00:54.649
The query takes 139 milliseconds, about fourteen nanoseconds a row.

13
00:00:54.833 --> 00:00:58.222
A loop written by hand needs a third of a nanosecond.

14
00:00:58.222 --> 00:01:04.041
The rest is machinery: calls through an interface, and an expression tree for every amount.

15
00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:11.823
Vectorized execution changes what next returns: a batch of 1,024 amounts in one plain array.

16
00:01:12.067 --> 00:01:16.374
The filter runs one tight loop over the array, with no calls inside.

17
00:01:16.374 --> 00:01:20.997
The compiler can even make it compare several amounts in one instruction.

18
00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:26.647
Now a call is paid once per batch: under ten thousand calls for ten million rows.

19
00:01:26.833 --> 00:01:31.277
The query now takes 19 milliseconds, about seven times faster.

20
00:01:31.467 --> 00:01:32.438
Why a thousand?

21
00:01:32.438 --> 00:01:35.353
One value per batch brings the overhead back.

22
00:01:35.353 --> 00:01:40.276
Above about eight thousand, a batch no longer fits in the processor's cache.

23
00:01:40.467 --> 00:01:44.031
DuckDB runs every query in batches of 2,048.

24
00:01:44.031 --> 00:01:50.592
PostgreSQL keeps one row at a time: its queries mostly fetch a few rows by index.

25
00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:53.081
A call per row is fine for ten rows.

26
00:01:53.081 --> 00:01:57.896
For ten million, pass a thousand at a time, so the time goes to the amounts.
